WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.150
- So, thanks, it's good to be here today.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:13.930
So, like Fran said, I've
worked for about eight years

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:17.110
in digital marketing and strategy

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:19.623
and there's so much
happening in the space.

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:22.850
When I first joined Weber,

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:25.660
every two years we would
reevaluate our offerings

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:26.493
and what we were doing,

00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:27.700
and then it seemed like, okay,

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.040
we're rethinking things every month,

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.660
and now it's like every six months

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.630
we're rethinking what we're offering.

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:35.310
And everything that's
on the screen right now,

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.190
we could spend the entire 50 minutes

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:39.420
talking about any of them.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.103
But today I'm gonna pick five of these

00:00:41.103 --> 00:00:46.103
that I think might be relevant
to Yale sort of holistically

00:00:47.254 --> 00:00:50.100
and I'm gonna talk about
them at a pretty high level

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:51.910
because of the amount
of time that we have,

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:53.370
so I'm gonna move pretty fast

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:56.193
and I will get you out there
for your drinks in time.

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.130
This is actually my second
time presenting here.

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:04.220
Last time I came I spoke
about content marketing

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:07.220
and how do you operationalize
a content marketing team,

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:08.670
and the biggest piece of feedback I got

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:10.870
was that I was speaking too
much from a corporate lens

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.800
and not enough for how things
could be applied at Yale.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.350
So at the end of every section

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:16.520
I have a couple of thought-starters

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:18.970
on what these trends might mean for Yale,

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:22.370
and hopefully a few of those
land and resonate with you.

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:25.453
If not, happy to answer
questions at the end.

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:30.450
So, the first thing that I
wanna start with today is

00:01:31.703 --> 00:01:33.233
a story about stories.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:35.540
The title of this presentation

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:37.240
is "Digital Trends: New, Now and Next".

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.250
This is very much a now category,

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:41.300
not something that's coming next.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.450
This is really something
that we are considering

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:46.330
a new best practice that
everyone has gotta grapple with

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:48.330
and have a really strong handle on.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:50.200
And when I say stories I don't mean

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:52.850
narrative and storytelling,

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:56.223
I mean stories as a technical
format for conveying content.

00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:59.050
So, one of the ways that I know

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:01.250
that I'm getting really old in this space

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:02.690
is that I now work with people

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:06.920
who have no recollection of
whole eras of the Internet

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:09.090
that were vital to my career,

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:10.190
and I think this is something

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:12.960
that is about to fall into that category

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.050
when I deal with some of the interns

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.680
and new people that we hire.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:19.360
If you think back 10 years ago

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.020
when smartphones were just achieving

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.670
some kind of a critical mass
of penetration in the market,

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:28.350
everyone was thinking about
how you do video on smartphones

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:29.940
and everyone was doing what they always do

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:30.773
with a new technology,

00:02:30.773 --> 00:02:33.060
they were taking the old format,

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:36.230
the old conventions from
film and TV and broadcast

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:38.530
and just slapping them
into the new format.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.270
And as a result we have a lot of people

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:41.970
really awkwardly flipping their phones

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:43.350
to view things sideways,

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:46.670
unfortunate incidents
when the sides of content

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:48.140
is getting cut out,

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:51.550
and it's very much a
not-native format of video

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:53.083
for the platform.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.950
Fast forward to today, times have changed.

00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:00.160
Whereas 10 years ago people
would not have even thought

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.640
about filming content vertically,

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.470
and in fact I've had many conversations

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:05.740
with film production people

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.880
where they don't want to
shoot content vertically

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:11.050
because it is so against the aesthetic

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:11.930
that they were trained with,

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:16.763
now people are very much embracing
that as a format overall.

00:03:18.641 --> 00:03:20.940
People are spending huge chunks of time

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:25.573
on mobile more so than desktop
more and more every year.

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:30.760
Stories are becoming the
true native application

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:32.630
of video on mobile.

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:35.230
And just looking around I'm guessing

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:37.200
that everyone has a pretty good idea

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.920
of what I mean when I say
stories, but just in case.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:44.920
Stories are a video format
that combines video components,

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:50.210
emoji overlays, typography,
additional graphics

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:55.460
into a series of static and
moving images that tell a story.

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:58.220
And just a quick little
example of what they're like.

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:01.520
This is one that Honda did.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:04.580
This is actually very
simple as far as stories go.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:07.440
It's just one video, there's
a few different overlays,

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.530
the emojis are playing a key role.

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:11.993
Some of them can actually
get quite complex.

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:16.180
And the story about stories is

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:19.820
that this is something
that started with Snapchat.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:22.960
This was one of the key
features that Snapchat used

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:26.010
as a differentiator against
other social networks,

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:29.410
Facebook, Twitter, Instagram,
that it was competing with.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:31.510
Facebook stole the format.

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:35.030
They integrated it into
the core Facebook app.

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:37.230
Most significantly they
made it a key piece

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:39.730
of Instagram's functionality.

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:41.900
That's one of the things
that caused Instagram

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:44.450
to explode in popularity
over the last few years.

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:47.430
At the same time they
kneecapped Snapchat's growth

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:50.350
and were able to keep them
as a second-tier competitor

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:52.360
rather than someone who could truly steal

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:54.160
a lot of market share from Facebook.

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:58.140
But Facebook isn't the
only that has taken it.

00:04:58.140 --> 00:04:59.990
Twitter has a version of this,

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:01.760
WhatsApp has a version of this.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:03.930
When we think about
across messaging platforms

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:07.830
and social platforms, the
use of stories is exploding.

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:10.340
Last year, in their Q4 reporting,

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:13.310
Instagram reported that on a daily basis,

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:16.130
half, 500 million of
their one billion users,

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:18.710
were using stories on a daily basis.

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:20.780
So they're either creating
stories themselves

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:22.230
or they're interacting with stories

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:24.670
that their friends have created.

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:29.670
So, when you think about
the types of content

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:32.510
that you need to be
sending out into the world

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:34.290
to reach your audiences,

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:36.030
stories is very quickly becoming

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:39.143
one of the dominant forms
across all social platforms.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:43.070
And so, very quickly,

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:45.910
what could this potentially mean for Yale?

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:50.360
If you are in charge with
running communications programs

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:53.040
that reach out to students
or prospective students,

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:55.590
that 100% means mobile and video,

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:57.810
and at this point I
assume most people know

00:05:57.810 --> 00:05:59.130
that those two things are vital

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:01.780
to any communications campaign
aimed at a younger audience,

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:03.000
but it also means stories.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:05.500
If you're just doing link share posts

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:07.700
or graphics or traditional video,

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:09.600
you're missing out on a huge opportunity

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:12.720
to talk to these audiences in a native way

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:13.970
and you really need to start thinking

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:16.470
about how you're going to
experiment with stories

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:18.400
over the next year.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:22.540
I think when people first see stories

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:24.830
and process what they are,

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:27.380
they go towards this place
of thinking of stories

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:31.890
as just a new evolution
of fast snackable content

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:35.430
for a generation that
has a shorter and shorter

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:36.770
attention span.

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:38.520
I don't think that's actually correct.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:40.240
Certainly there are some applications

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:42.640
that fall into that realm,

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:45.140
but if you look at what
news organizations are doing

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:47.603
on Instagram, on Facebook, on Snapchat.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:52.130
Institutions like Vox are doing
really interesting things,

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:55.220
taking the ability of stories

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:59.220
to string multiple different
content pieces together.

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:03.590
So, think opening with a
title card moving to a video,

00:07:03.590 --> 00:07:05.760
moving into a graphic of some kind,

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:08.770
moving into a type of graphic
statement back to a video.

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:11.620
And they're actually
finding really engaging

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:13.730
long-form uses for stories

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:16.640
where you can talk about
really complex topics

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:18.820
through a content format

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:20.280
that maybe you weren't quite thinking

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:23.740
would be able to bear
that much information.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:26.290
So, when you think about how
you might use stories this year

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:30.680
don't just think about
students and brand affinity

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:34.710
and raising awareness and short,
really quick snappy things.

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:36.390
Think about how it might also apply

00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:40.500
to longer form, more complex
topics that you want to convey

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:42.510
but you want to get out to audiences

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:43.760
on all of the different channels,

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:46.950
not just in a written text
or the types of formats

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:49.250
that you would usually
think of for long form.

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:53.883
Live streaming is the new social.

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:59.240
When I got into digital marketing

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:00.840
and advertising and strategy,

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:03.760
it was the golden era of blogs,

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:07.490
so I'm very much of the techno utopian

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:09.400
democratization of everything.

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:10.800
Not that I believe any of that anymore

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:12.690
after the last two years.

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:15.770
But I think what we saw happen was

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:18.700
as blogging and those technologies faded,

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:20.380
social came onto the scene.

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:22.280
You have Mark Zuckerberg talking about

00:08:23.427 --> 00:08:25.370
the way that Facebook and social

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:27.860
facilitates community
building and gathering,

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:31.110
and I think we've all seen
that that is not actually true

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:32.690
in a lot of instances lately.

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:34.340
But one of the things that
I'm really interested in

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:35.420
is the way the there are

00:08:35.420 --> 00:08:37.690
some new applications of live streaming

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:40.860
that may actually be able to bring back

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:45.150
just a little bit of that
hope that there could be

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:48.613
a true community platform
enabled by technology.

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:51.810
So, before we get into
what that actually is,

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:53.330
some background.

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:54.330
Over the last year,

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:58.660
streaming and over-the-top
delivery systems for media

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:02.090
have started to surpass
paid TV and broadcast

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:04.533
in terms of total number of subscribers.

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:09.900
If you look at the growth of
cord-cutters and cord-nevers,

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:12.450
these are people who
have either gotten rid

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:15.530
of their traditional TV and cable packages

00:09:15.530 --> 00:09:17.890
or have never actually
had cable and TV packages

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:20.050
'cause they're getting
it all over the Internet.

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:22.240
These are continuing grow.

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:25.270
And these types of streaming applications

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:27.760
are becoming the dominant
way that people get

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:29.310
what we have traditionally thought of

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:32.273
as programs, televised media.

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:36.400
So, these platforms are starting to gain

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:38.640
a majority share of the market

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:41.600
over the traditional vehicles.

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:42.610
At the same time,

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:44.870
they're also starting
to dominate creatively.

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:46.840
So, last year was the first year

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:51.423
where the streaming services,
Amazon, Hulu, Netflix,

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:55.640
actually commissioned and
produced more TV shows

00:09:55.640 --> 00:09:58.180
than all the broadcast networks combined.

00:09:58.180 --> 00:09:59.470
So, we're now at a place

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:01.580
where the dynamic has completely flipped

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:05.820
and streaming is now the
dominant and natural way

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:09.433
that people are taking
in that type of media.

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:13.520
Social platforms are seeing this.

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:15.810
It's been no secret that
for a really long time

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:18.740
Facebook's goal from an
advertising perspective

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:21.010
has been to steal away the dollars

00:10:21.010 --> 00:10:23.330
that would traditionally go to television

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:26.380
and realize that as its own revenue.

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:30.070
We've seen over the last
year a number of platforms,

00:10:30.070 --> 00:10:33.100
Facebook, Instagram, all trying to launch

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:36.630
their own version of streaming television.

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:39.650
And if you take a look
at total number of users,

00:10:39.650 --> 00:10:42.110
which is what this chart shows right now,

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:43.760
it seems like they're winning.

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:46.940
There's more people who are streaming

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:48.770
and watching that type of program

00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:52.210
plus pure, raw,
user-created live streaming

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:56.210
than on digital streaming
subscription services,

00:10:56.210 --> 00:10:59.830
TV networks or site apps or
even gaming applications.

00:10:59.830 --> 00:11:01.770
But this is all a lie, actually.

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:04.160
This data is fuzzy.

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:06.860
Facebook would love for
you to believe it's true

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:09.213
but it's only part of the story.

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:13.640
Even though they have
more people by volume

00:11:13.640 --> 00:11:15.520
than some channels,

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:17.240
I think the future of live streaming

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:19.820
isn't necessarily going
to be the social platform,

00:11:19.820 --> 00:11:21.360
it's going to be Twitch.

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:25.240
It's going to be places
like Netflix, YouTube, Hulu,

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:28.410
they're actually getting a
lot more time and engagement

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:29.570
with these audiences

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:33.050
than the Facebooks and the
Instagrams of the world.

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:35.520
And Twitch in particular

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:38.150
I think is poised to
dominate in this space,

00:11:38.150 --> 00:11:41.140
not necessarily on the
programmed media side

00:11:41.140 --> 00:11:44.713
but on the raw, live
stream, user-created side.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:48.660
So, for those of you who
don't know what Twitch is,

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:51.300
Twitch is a live streaming platform

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:53.720
that's available on all devices.

00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:56.350
It's made its reputation
on the back of e-sports.

00:11:56.350 --> 00:12:00.060
People who play video
games professionally go on,

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:04.080
play each other, stream
themselves playing these games,

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:08.160
and while they're playing
there's a running live chat

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:09.820
with all the people who follow them

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.320
and they're engaging with
conversation with those people.

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:13.340
They're reading the comments,

00:12:13.340 --> 00:12:15.820
those people are
interacting with each other,

00:12:15.820 --> 00:12:16.980
with the streamer.

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:20.690
There's some really
interesting subscription

00:12:20.690 --> 00:12:22.860
and incentive infrastructure built in

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:25.300
to allow people to support creators

00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:26.780
and pay them and tip them.

00:12:26.780 --> 00:12:28.260
There's interesting incentives

00:12:28.260 --> 00:12:29.970
that the creators and streamers

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:32.330
can send back down into the community

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:34.030
to support their loyal followers.

00:12:34.030 --> 00:12:36.730
And what we've seen over
the past few years is

00:12:36.730 --> 00:12:39.010
not only are people spending

00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:41.240
huge amounts of time on this platform,

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:43.340
there are genuine communities forming

00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:45.970
around these games,
around these streamers,

00:12:45.970 --> 00:12:49.570
that for the moment don't
have some of the same problems

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:53.230
that we're seeing in terms
of community formation

00:12:53.230 --> 00:12:57.110
and public discussion and
bullying issues, trolls,

00:12:57.110 --> 00:12:59.960
people coming in to poison the dialogue.

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:03.253
For the moment that hasn't
really happened at Twitch.

00:13:04.550 --> 00:13:07.640
But at the same time I think
even though that's how Twitch

00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:10.370
has become the platform that it is,

00:13:10.370 --> 00:13:13.410
that's not what Twitch sees
as the future of the platform.

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:15.090
It's always gonna be an important piece,

00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:17.360
but Twitch itself wants to become

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:20.210
the platform for all live streaming

00:13:20.210 --> 00:13:22.020
for a whole variety of communities,

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:27.020
from musicians to people
who right now are doing

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:30.950
beauty blogging on Instagram or YouTube,

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:33.830
DIY, politics, travel.

00:13:33.830 --> 00:13:34.880
Twitch wants to be the place

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:38.530
where anyone who has a
use for live streaming

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:39.960
and wants to build a community

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:42.960
can come and make that
platform their home.

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:47.310
And so far we're actually
seeing some success there.

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:49.040
There's been really
interesting experiments

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:51.290
by The Washington Post
and news organizations

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:55.770
to jump on the platform and
create their own streams.

00:13:55.770 --> 00:13:58.220
Amateur musicians are
coming on the platform

00:13:58.220 --> 00:13:59.360
and building their own communities

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:02.690
and finding ways to support
creation of their songs.

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:05.579
Nike and other brands are
coming onto the platform

00:14:05.579 --> 00:14:08.100
to both support the e-gamers

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:10.750
and piggyback off of the
streams that they're doing

00:14:10.750 --> 00:14:12.930
but also find new and interesting ways

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:15.210
to run their own streams on the platform.

00:14:15.210 --> 00:14:17.570
And as we get into the 2020 election cycle

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:21.400
I think there's going to be
no shortage of live events

00:14:21.400 --> 00:14:22.590
that are right for streaming

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:24.540
and I think it's gonna be
really interesting to see

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:26.470
if Twitch can have a breakout moment

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:28.210
in the politics and news space

00:14:28.210 --> 00:14:30.050
and not just the e-sports community.

00:14:30.050 --> 00:14:32.670
And when that happens I think
it'll be interesting to see

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:35.630
can they actually deal
with all of the problems

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:36.850
that we've seen on social media

00:14:36.850 --> 00:14:38.173
that are inevitably gonna get poured over

00:14:38.173 --> 00:14:39.763
into that platform as well.

00:14:41.170 --> 00:14:42.870
So, what does this mean for Yale?

00:14:42.870 --> 00:14:45.570
Start thinking about a live
content streaming strategy.

00:14:45.570 --> 00:14:47.560
I'm sure you guys have
live streaming strategies,

00:14:47.560 --> 00:14:49.860
you guys do tons of events like this.

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:52.730
I'm sure there's no shortage
of streams happening

00:14:52.730 --> 00:14:54.550
on owned channels for Yale.

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:58.170
But what is your live
streaming channel strategy

00:14:58.170 --> 00:14:59.860
off Yale channels?

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:01.720
What would you do if you had to go

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:03.830
and leverage something like Twitch

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:05.630
in order to meet an
audience where they are

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:07.423
and not expect them to come to you?

00:15:08.310 --> 00:15:09.700
I think over the next year

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:12.950
everyone should be watching
Twitch to see what happens.

00:15:12.950 --> 00:15:16.200
There's going to be so many
experiments on the platform

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:18.870
by brands, by politicians.

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:21.340
It's gonna be really interesting to see

00:15:21.340 --> 00:15:23.610
where they can break
through and what takes off

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:27.350
versus is it gonna just be a blip

00:15:27.350 --> 00:15:30.100
and be purely relegated
to this e-sports space.

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:33.180
And lastly, as you think
about your strategy,

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:35.210
don't just think, oh, I'm gonna go

00:15:35.210 --> 00:15:36.990
and create my own live stream on Twitch.

00:15:36.990 --> 00:15:40.050
There are lots of interesting
creators on the platform

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:43.400
and there's lots of ways to
piggyback off of those creators.

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:46.270
I think one of the more interesting
things that I've seen is

00:15:46.270 --> 00:15:48.930
climate scientists have
actually been piggybacking

00:15:48.930 --> 00:15:50.800
onto the live stream of gamers.

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:55.800
They'll talk to someone
who has a following,

00:15:56.260 --> 00:15:58.570
they'll play a game
like Fortnite with them,

00:15:58.570 --> 00:16:01.530
and they will talk about
climate change science

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:04.210
with the gamer while
they're playing Fortnite

00:16:04.210 --> 00:16:05.340
so that they're able to reach

00:16:05.340 --> 00:16:06.700
that broader community of people

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:10.363
who are used to following that streamer.

00:16:13.330 --> 00:16:16.530
Number three, rise of
the machine interfaces.

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:20.270
So, I think the big headline point here is

00:16:20.270 --> 00:16:22.790
we're living through probably
one of the biggest changes

00:16:22.790 --> 00:16:24.780
in user interfaces in 30 years.

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:28.000
If you think back we had the introduction

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:29.870
of the graphical user interface.

00:16:29.870 --> 00:16:31.890
Things were pretty static for a long time.

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:33.050
We got mobile.

00:16:33.050 --> 00:16:34.630
Mobile for the longest time

00:16:34.630 --> 00:16:36.100
didn't even have its own native formats,

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:37.270
we're just getting to that now,

00:16:37.270 --> 00:16:40.500
it was just a reapplication
of what we had from desktops

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:42.280
into a smaller screen.

00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:44.400
But now we're actually starting to see

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:46.750
much more native applications

00:16:46.750 --> 00:16:49.960
of how we deal with a mobile-first world,

00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:52.630
and I wanna talk through
a few of those things,

00:16:52.630 --> 00:16:55.240
a few of those interfaces
and what they mean.

00:16:55.240 --> 00:16:57.550
But first I think it's
important to note that

00:16:59.830 --> 00:17:03.150
messaging and one-to-one or
one-to-few communications

00:17:03.150 --> 00:17:06.130
has started to overtake social media.

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:08.200
So, what you're seeing here,

00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:10.410
the big four messaging platforms

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:12.110
back in I think it was, yeah, 2015

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:16.360
crossed over and have more daily users

00:17:16.360 --> 00:17:17.940
than the social platforms,

00:17:17.940 --> 00:17:21.363
and that is growing and is
probably going to keep growing.

00:17:24.010 --> 00:17:27.480
This is driven in many ways by Asia

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:31.200
but we in the West are starting
to catch up more and more.

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:32.870
And I think nowhere do you see this more

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:36.230
than in the way Facebook
is pivoting right now.

00:17:36.230 --> 00:17:37.530
If you think about all the things

00:17:37.530 --> 00:17:38.830
that they've announced lately,

00:17:38.830 --> 00:17:42.260
one the most significant is
that they're going to integrate

00:17:43.185 --> 00:17:46.140
WhatsApp, Messenger, and
Instagram on the backend

00:17:46.140 --> 00:17:50.220
to essentially create one
interoperable messaging system.

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:52.580
And in part they're doing that

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:56.410
because those messaging platforms
are private and encrypted,

00:17:56.410 --> 00:17:58.680
and if more people are moving over

00:17:58.680 --> 00:18:01.400
towards messaging platforms,
Facebook wants to be there.

00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:04.470
And if that can be in a
private and encrypted format,

00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:05.700
Facebook doesn't have to deal

00:18:05.700 --> 00:18:08.400
with all of the content
challenges that it has right now

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:09.670
'cause it can't see the content

00:18:09.670 --> 00:18:10.840
that's going over those networks.

00:18:10.840 --> 00:18:12.560
So if people are bullying,

00:18:12.560 --> 00:18:14.350
if there's misinformation campaigns,

00:18:14.350 --> 00:18:16.920
Facebook is technically
unable to even intervene

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:18.970
in the way that a lot of
the policy discussions

00:18:18.970 --> 00:18:20.800
are happening right now.

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:22.123
So that's one reason.

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:25.740
It's gonna solve the moderation
challenges they have.

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:27.940
But the other piece is that they see

00:18:27.940 --> 00:18:29.620
the way that user habits are changing.

00:18:29.620 --> 00:18:34.620
They look at WeeChat in Asia
and see how it has become

00:18:35.490 --> 00:18:38.290
the one platform where everything happens.

00:18:38.290 --> 00:18:40.310
If you're a user of WeeChat in Asia,

00:18:40.310 --> 00:18:44.010
you can use it to order
food, you can get your car,

00:18:44.010 --> 00:18:46.340
you can order a movie or plane tickets,

00:18:46.340 --> 00:18:48.650
you can shop, you can
chat with your friends.

00:18:48.650 --> 00:18:51.950
Everything is plugged
into the messaging system

00:18:51.950 --> 00:18:53.780
and everything, including commerce,

00:18:53.780 --> 00:18:55.470
can happen through that platform.

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:57.380
Facebook wants to be the
one that's in the position

00:18:57.380 --> 00:19:00.780
to be the WeeChat of the West

00:19:00.780 --> 00:19:02.960
and I think that's also
another reason you see them

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:04.820
starting to dip their toe
into the crypto space.

00:19:04.820 --> 00:19:05.700
It would be great if they could have

00:19:05.700 --> 00:19:07.900
their own currency within the platform

00:19:07.900 --> 00:19:10.530
to help negotiate all
of those transactions.

00:19:10.530 --> 00:19:12.700
But this is actually a big problem

00:19:12.700 --> 00:19:16.613
for brands, corporations,
institutions like Yale.

00:19:17.530 --> 00:19:20.280
We do not have as advanced
the messaging platform

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:22.100
as they do in Asia here,

00:19:22.100 --> 00:19:23.920
and if you're trying to reach people

00:19:23.920 --> 00:19:26.790
on these messaging
platforms, it's really hard.

00:19:26.790 --> 00:19:29.680
It is not like a plug-and-play add system

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:33.210
or just being able to get up organically

00:19:33.210 --> 00:19:35.400
and hope people find you on the platform.

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:39.130
Those opportunities are not
nearly as robust in messaging

00:19:39.130 --> 00:19:40.650
here as they are in Asia,

00:19:40.650 --> 00:19:43.400
so that is a conundrum
that a lot of brands

00:19:43.400 --> 00:19:45.250
are gonna have to start dealing with.

00:19:46.720 --> 00:19:49.890
Pivoting, I promise this
is not a non sequitur.

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:53.580
At the same time, we're
seeing AI begin to transform

00:19:53.580 --> 00:19:56.410
whole industries and
sectors of the economy.

00:19:56.410 --> 00:19:58.780
At Weber Shandwick we
really looked at this

00:19:58.780 --> 00:20:00.960
as an analytics opportunity first,

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:03.630
and the chart that you
see here from McKinsey

00:20:03.630 --> 00:20:08.360
is actually sizing the
analytics opportunity of AI.

00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:10.110
I know it's hard to read, I'll spare you.

00:20:10.110 --> 00:20:13.250
The upshot is hundreds
of billions of dollars

00:20:13.250 --> 00:20:15.880
in potential value to
be unlocked through AI

00:20:15.880 --> 00:20:18.420
just in the analytics
space across industries.

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:20.870
But when you think about
marketing and communications

00:20:20.870 --> 00:20:23.790
inclusive of but beyond analytics,

00:20:23.790 --> 00:20:27.460
McKinsey thinks there's a
$1-2 trillion opportunity

00:20:27.460 --> 00:20:29.990
to be unlocked there as well.

00:20:29.990 --> 00:20:33.670
And that's everything from
ways that AI can assist

00:20:33.670 --> 00:20:36.510
with better content personalization,

00:20:36.510 --> 00:20:39.023
upgrading service functions.

00:20:40.050 --> 00:20:42.830
There are lots of ways that AI

00:20:42.830 --> 00:20:44.880
can aid in communication plans.

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:46.650
So, why am I talking about that

00:20:46.650 --> 00:20:48.800
and what is the connection to messaging?

00:20:48.800 --> 00:20:50.163
It's chatbots.

00:20:51.116 --> 00:20:54.920
What we're seeing right now
is that AI-driven chatbots

00:20:54.920 --> 00:20:56.680
are really the initial touchpoint

00:20:56.680 --> 00:20:58.690
where these two trends are coming together

00:20:58.690 --> 00:21:02.650
in a way that's really
impactful for our clients.

00:21:02.650 --> 00:21:05.790
So, more and more consumers
want to interact with brands

00:21:05.790 --> 00:21:07.300
through messaging systems.

00:21:07.300 --> 00:21:09.810
They don't wanna deal with phone trees

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:11.980
and terrible personal customer service.

00:21:11.980 --> 00:21:14.100
They don't wanna go and navigate websites

00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:17.620
where the logic isn't
constructed according to the way

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:19.130
that they want to interact with the brand

00:21:19.130 --> 00:21:21.020
or how their questions
need to be answered.

00:21:21.020 --> 00:21:24.250
They want a very simple system
where they can ask questions,

00:21:24.250 --> 00:21:25.450
questions can be answered,

00:21:25.450 --> 00:21:27.330
and they can get to the thing
that they need to get to

00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:28.930
as fast as possible.

00:21:28.930 --> 00:21:31.290
And chatbots, particularly chatbots

00:21:31.290 --> 00:21:34.800
that are leveraging natural
language processing in AI

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:36.730
to understand intent and sentiment

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:40.310
and connect that back up to a
potential bank of responses,

00:21:40.310 --> 00:21:43.290
those are the places where we're seeing

00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:45.240
really successful engagements

00:21:45.240 --> 00:21:48.520
and a really successful application

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:50.820
in bringing AI and messaging together

00:21:50.820 --> 00:21:53.810
in a way that solves
that messaging challenge

00:21:53.810 --> 00:21:55.520
in terms of how you reach people

00:21:55.520 --> 00:21:57.120
through that type of a platform.

00:21:59.260 --> 00:22:04.100
And even the best chatbot
is still going to be,

00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:06.920
for now at least, screen and text-based.

00:22:06.920 --> 00:22:08.610
But that is also not the way

00:22:08.610 --> 00:22:09.990
that things are trending overall

00:22:09.990 --> 00:22:11.790
when we think about user interfaces.

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:14.920
We're seeing more and more

00:22:14.920 --> 00:22:18.030
people are interacting through voice.

00:22:18.030 --> 00:22:20.790
So, right now more than
1/2 of the US population

00:22:20.790 --> 00:22:24.280
actively uses voice assistance
either through their phone

00:22:24.280 --> 00:22:26.930
or through a smart speaker in the home.

00:22:26.930 --> 00:22:27.950
Excuse me.

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:30.590
And this is starting to radically alter

00:22:30.590 --> 00:22:31.620
both the search market

00:22:31.620 --> 00:22:34.210
and the way people expect to interact

00:22:34.210 --> 00:22:36.230
with technology and with brands.

00:22:36.230 --> 00:22:39.170
So, Comscore estimates that by next year

00:22:39.170 --> 00:22:41.940
50% of all searches are
gonna be done by voice.

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:44.380
And Gartner says by the end of next year

00:22:44.380 --> 00:22:46.870
30% of all those searches
are gonna be on a device

00:22:46.870 --> 00:22:49.620
that does not have a screen at all.

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:52.063
So, that creates another
set of challenges.

00:22:52.990 --> 00:22:55.220
If you think about what
a search strategy is,

00:22:55.220 --> 00:22:59.030
how do you make sure that when
people are asking questions

00:22:59.030 --> 00:23:00.560
to a voice assistant,

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:02.980
your results are getting surfaced?

00:23:02.980 --> 00:23:05.820
How do you create applications

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:09.480
that can reach and engage
people on those platforms?

00:23:09.480 --> 00:23:10.313
We're starting to see

00:23:10.313 --> 00:23:12.992
some interesting things
happen in that space.

00:23:12.992 --> 00:23:17.610
Two in particular that seem
to be successful so far

00:23:17.610 --> 00:23:19.563
is customer service.

00:23:20.880 --> 00:23:23.560
We've seen some companies,
particularly energy utilities,

00:23:23.560 --> 00:23:26.250
put together customer service applications

00:23:26.250 --> 00:23:28.840
that can be activated by
voice on home speakers

00:23:28.840 --> 00:23:31.090
and walk you through any
problem that you're having,

00:23:31.090 --> 00:23:32.750
and they've also been
using it for bill pay.

00:23:32.750 --> 00:23:36.570
So, very common customer interactions,

00:23:36.570 --> 00:23:39.650
they're going out, finding
new ways to get people

00:23:39.650 --> 00:23:42.380
through what can often
be frustrating processes

00:23:42.380 --> 00:23:44.293
faster using new technology.

00:23:46.510 --> 00:23:48.990
And so, what does that mean at Yale?

00:23:48.990 --> 00:23:51.610
I think it's a few very
disparate things, actually.

00:23:51.610 --> 00:23:56.430
So, I'm sure a lot of you are
in charge of social strategy

00:23:56.430 --> 00:23:59.170
for different functions within Yale.

00:23:59.170 --> 00:24:01.510
So, how is your social
strategy going to evolve

00:24:01.510 --> 00:24:05.720
as audiences stop using social
and move over to messaging?

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:08.770
What are you gonna do when
it's harder to reach them

00:24:08.770 --> 00:24:11.380
because the platforms are less open,

00:24:11.380 --> 00:24:13.863
less conducive to mass communication?

00:24:15.090 --> 00:24:18.680
When we think about
leveraging AI, chatbots,

00:24:18.680 --> 00:24:20.530
what are you doing on a regular basis

00:24:20.530 --> 00:24:23.760
that can be routinized and automated?

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:25.440
What are the frequently asked questions

00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:27.830
that your department gets all of the time

00:24:27.830 --> 00:24:30.660
that are either buried in web pages

00:24:30.660 --> 00:24:34.610
or take an inordinate amount
of a real person's time

00:24:34.610 --> 00:24:37.620
to run down when they're
getting them all the time?

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:41.290
These are places where some
kind of automated solution

00:24:41.290 --> 00:24:45.930
like a chatbot or a messaging application

00:24:45.930 --> 00:24:48.470
can actually solve those problems,

00:24:48.470 --> 00:24:50.610
give you a better experience to users,

00:24:50.610 --> 00:24:54.630
and free up your staff time
to do more strategic things

00:24:54.630 --> 00:24:57.700
or take the people who really
need the one-on-one help

00:24:57.700 --> 00:25:00.240
and go a lot deeper and
have a much higher level

00:25:00.240 --> 00:25:03.190
of that personal customer
service over fewer interactions

00:25:03.190 --> 00:25:04.690
rather than spreading themselves thin

00:25:04.690 --> 00:25:06.520
over a large number of interactions.

00:25:06.520 --> 00:25:09.370
And lastly, how is your
SEO strategy gonna evolve

00:25:10.383 --> 00:25:13.350
as voice captures more and
more of the search market?

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:17.553
It's not gonna be about
optimizing around keywords,

00:25:19.220 --> 00:25:23.940
to the extent that your pages
are constructed correctly.

00:25:23.940 --> 00:25:26.970
That always helps but
people are asking questions.

00:25:26.970 --> 00:25:28.410
Right now they're asking questions

00:25:28.410 --> 00:25:31.420
about the weather and news and sports

00:25:31.420 --> 00:25:34.340
but soon they're gonna be asking
questions about everything.

00:25:34.340 --> 00:25:37.980
And are you ready to get
served in the top result

00:25:37.980 --> 00:25:40.860
for a particular question
that is gonna be relevant

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:42.710
to you as an institution

00:25:42.710 --> 00:25:44.710
and the people that you wanna talk with?

00:25:47.370 --> 00:25:49.260
So, fake everything.

00:25:49.260 --> 00:25:51.460
This one's probably
pretty self-explanatory.

00:25:52.540 --> 00:25:56.030
I think we've all seen the
issues over the past years.

00:25:56.030 --> 00:26:00.210
We have Russian bots manipulating
political conversations.

00:26:00.210 --> 00:26:03.363
We have bots in fraud on the ad side.

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:06.410
We have the potential for deepfakes

00:26:06.410 --> 00:26:08.560
to disrupt political conversation,

00:26:08.560 --> 00:26:11.270
or we are one deepfake away

00:26:11.270 --> 00:26:14.720
from a company's bottom
line getting tanked.

00:26:14.720 --> 00:26:17.060
And even the stuff we saw

00:26:17.060 --> 00:26:19.630
around Nancy Pelosi just a few weeks ago

00:26:19.630 --> 00:26:21.870
with the altered video, what
people would call a cheap fake,

00:26:21.870 --> 00:26:23.970
it's not even a real deepfake,

00:26:23.970 --> 00:26:25.330
it's just been slightly altered,

00:26:25.330 --> 00:26:27.693
can cause a huge amount of problem.

00:26:28.760 --> 00:26:32.130
And we're even seeing some
interesting things happen,

00:26:32.130 --> 00:26:32.970
it's not all negative,

00:26:32.970 --> 00:26:34.260
we're seeing some interesting

00:26:34.260 --> 00:26:36.150
positive fake things happening too,

00:26:36.150 --> 00:26:39.580
like the growth of what people
are calling fake influencers

00:26:39.580 --> 00:26:44.580
but are actually almost like
digital AI mascots for brands.

00:26:46.060 --> 00:26:49.750
And these are just a few
influencers that are not real

00:26:49.750 --> 00:26:53.673
but are just as influential
as many celebrities are today.

00:26:55.590 --> 00:27:00.110
So, there's a lot going on in this space.

00:27:00.110 --> 00:27:02.440
It probably feels like
we've reached peak fake

00:27:02.440 --> 00:27:04.580
but I think it's actually
gonna get a lot worse

00:27:04.580 --> 00:27:06.163
before it gets better.

00:27:07.391 --> 00:27:10.913
And that's a challenge for our
clients and for institutions.

00:27:12.010 --> 00:27:15.260
In this environment,
current listening solutions

00:27:15.260 --> 00:27:17.560
are pretty inadequate.

00:27:17.560 --> 00:27:19.280
For the most part when you ask people,

00:27:19.280 --> 00:27:20.940
how are you monitoring
what people are saying?

00:27:20.940 --> 00:27:24.650
How are you able to know whether
something is real or fake?

00:27:24.650 --> 00:27:26.980
People will tell you, "Oh,
we're using Brandwatch,"

00:27:26.980 --> 00:27:30.750
or, "We're using Sysomos
or Crimson Hexagon."

00:27:30.750 --> 00:27:33.980
And that's great, but are
they going deep enough

00:27:33.980 --> 00:27:37.660
to really understand what is happening

00:27:37.660 --> 00:27:39.320
and where conversations are coming from?

00:27:39.320 --> 00:27:41.350
'Cause conversations
are not just happening

00:27:41.350 --> 00:27:46.090
on Facebook and Twitter
and YouTube and Google,

00:27:46.090 --> 00:27:49.800
there is whole other
layers to the Internet

00:27:49.800 --> 00:27:53.520
and conversations are
starting way down deep,

00:27:53.520 --> 00:27:55.220
hidden away from most people,

00:27:55.220 --> 00:27:57.160
and they are percolating up

00:27:57.160 --> 00:28:01.963
before you see them go
viral on the main networks.

00:28:03.390 --> 00:28:06.860
And the challenge is by the
time it gets to the main network

00:28:06.860 --> 00:28:10.293
if you are a company that is,

00:28:13.300 --> 00:28:15.600
if you are a company that is
being attacked in some way,

00:28:15.600 --> 00:28:17.330
if you're a politician or a government

00:28:17.330 --> 00:28:18.330
that's being attacked,

00:28:18.330 --> 00:28:20.540
by the time it gets to the main networks,

00:28:20.540 --> 00:28:23.230
it's too late, all you can do is react.

00:28:23.230 --> 00:28:26.650
You have no chance of
limiting the conversation,

00:28:26.650 --> 00:28:29.763
redefining the framework
of the conversation.

00:28:30.760 --> 00:28:34.330
Your ability to proactively
dampen the effect

00:28:34.330 --> 00:28:35.950
is already gone.

00:28:35.950 --> 00:28:38.920
So, the thing that we're
seeing companies do

00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:39.840
in response to this

00:28:39.840 --> 00:28:42.530
is really level up their
listening capabilities,

00:28:42.530 --> 00:28:44.430
and the way that they're doing that

00:28:44.430 --> 00:28:46.830
is what we're calling media forensics.

00:28:46.830 --> 00:28:48.570
It's a set of processes and tools,

00:28:48.570 --> 00:28:52.840
it's a combination of AI and machines

00:28:52.840 --> 00:28:56.590
with human experts who
can do real research

00:28:56.590 --> 00:28:59.260
and understand what the
machines are uncovering

00:28:59.260 --> 00:29:02.060
and going to places that the
machines can't actually go.

00:29:02.990 --> 00:29:05.630
If you think back to all
of the different platforms,

00:29:05.630 --> 00:29:07.300
the iceberg in that slide,

00:29:07.300 --> 00:29:09.440
a lot of them have APIs
that can be scraped

00:29:09.440 --> 00:29:12.000
by listening services,
a lot of them don't.

00:29:12.000 --> 00:29:14.580
A lot of them you have to
have a real person inside

00:29:14.580 --> 00:29:18.450
who understands the
cultures of the platform

00:29:18.450 --> 00:29:19.780
investigating what's happening.

00:29:19.780 --> 00:29:23.130
And we're seeing companies
bring these two things together

00:29:23.130 --> 00:29:25.350
to start to understand

00:29:26.790 --> 00:29:29.960
not just where things come
from and how they've spread

00:29:29.960 --> 00:29:32.980
but also as a warning system
that something may be coming

00:29:32.980 --> 00:29:34.910
and they're going to need
to have a response to it

00:29:34.910 --> 00:29:35.773
in some way.

00:29:36.840 --> 00:29:40.030
Just a quick example of how
this has worked in the past.

00:29:40.030 --> 00:29:43.240
So, everyone probably
recognizes Emma Gonzalez,

00:29:43.240 --> 00:29:45.830
the Parkland's activist.

00:29:45.830 --> 00:29:48.480
She was on a Teen Vogue cover shoot

00:29:48.480 --> 00:29:52.350
with a few of her fellow activists

00:29:52.350 --> 00:29:57.170
and she was shown ripping up
this picture of a gun target.

00:29:57.170 --> 00:30:00.738
And way down in some forums

00:30:00.738 --> 00:30:04.430
that gun right advocates congregate in,

00:30:04.430 --> 00:30:06.840
they photoshopped the target

00:30:06.840 --> 00:30:08.730
into a picture of the Constitution.

00:30:08.730 --> 00:30:10.310
They started to spread that privately

00:30:10.310 --> 00:30:11.610
through their own networks.

00:30:11.610 --> 00:30:15.200
It started to percolate up into
Reddit and some other places

00:30:15.200 --> 00:30:18.070
that then people took it up

00:30:18.070 --> 00:30:20.310
and spread it into Twitter, Facebook,

00:30:20.310 --> 00:30:22.670
and eventually it blew
up and got on the news.

00:30:22.670 --> 00:30:26.050
So, it's just an example
of how misinformation

00:30:26.050 --> 00:30:28.090
can percolate through all of those levels,

00:30:28.090 --> 00:30:30.030
and media forensics is a way to

00:30:31.130 --> 00:30:33.580
if necessary backtrack through those steps

00:30:33.580 --> 00:30:35.310
and understand what happened

00:30:35.310 --> 00:30:37.830
or hopefully proactively see it

00:30:37.830 --> 00:30:40.703
before it breaks out
and (mumbles) respond.

00:30:42.040 --> 00:30:46.700
So, it is very much a crisis
mitigation and prevention tool

00:30:46.700 --> 00:30:48.040
but I think it also goes beyond that.

00:30:48.040 --> 00:30:51.900
So, more and more consumers
are pushing companies

00:30:51.900 --> 00:30:53.850
to take stands on social issues.

00:30:53.850 --> 00:30:56.050
People want to be engaged with companies

00:30:56.050 --> 00:30:59.260
that are active socially and politically,

00:30:59.260 --> 00:31:02.360
and this is putting
companies in a bit of a bind.

00:31:02.360 --> 00:31:04.550
There's lots of hashtags and memes

00:31:04.550 --> 00:31:08.180
and things that are flying
around on social all of the time.

00:31:08.180 --> 00:31:10.450
How do you know whether something is right

00:31:10.450 --> 00:31:12.440
for you to jump onto or not?

00:31:12.440 --> 00:31:15.080
Do you understand the
full provenance of an idea

00:31:15.080 --> 00:31:17.720
that is going viral on the web?

00:31:17.720 --> 00:31:20.400
And one of the best examples
that I've ever seen of this is

00:31:20.400 --> 00:31:22.110
this reporting by The New York Times.

00:31:22.110 --> 00:31:25.230
If you haven't read it I highly
recommend you go and find it

00:31:25.230 --> 00:31:26.490
and take a look at it later today.

00:31:26.490 --> 00:31:28.490
It takes like 10 minutes to get through.

00:31:29.432 --> 00:31:32.060
It's less intimidating
than it looks at times.

00:31:32.060 --> 00:31:35.367
But what they did was took
a look at this phrase,

00:31:35.367 --> 00:31:39.130
"jobs not mobs," and they
traced the evolution of it

00:31:39.130 --> 00:31:42.930
from right-wing forums over onto Reddit

00:31:42.930 --> 00:31:46.320
where it was picked up by Scott Adams,

00:31:46.320 --> 00:31:48.370
the cartoonist who created Dilbert,

00:31:48.370 --> 00:31:51.730
promoted onto his social channels,

00:31:51.730 --> 00:31:55.030
picked up by Fox News and
then eventually tweeted out

00:31:55.030 --> 00:31:56.992
by the President of the United States.

00:31:56.992 --> 00:32:01.430
So, again, it's about
understanding the full context

00:32:01.430 --> 00:32:04.470
of where something comes from
and how the conversations

00:32:04.470 --> 00:32:06.560
that we're having as a
society are developing

00:32:06.560 --> 00:32:09.250
so that you can evaluate
whether or not it makes sense

00:32:09.250 --> 00:32:12.100
for you to be in that conversation or not

00:32:12.100 --> 00:32:13.790
or at least have a game plan

00:32:13.790 --> 00:32:16.230
for responding to that conversation

00:32:16.230 --> 00:32:19.243
if it's something that's directed
at you as an organization.

00:32:20.910 --> 00:32:22.220
And so, what it means at Yale?

00:32:22.220 --> 00:32:24.240
I think this is very much in line

00:32:24.240 --> 00:32:25.820
with what we typically tell our clients.

00:32:25.820 --> 00:32:29.300
So, what is your monitoring
solution right now?

00:32:29.300 --> 00:32:30.740
How deep does it go?

00:32:30.740 --> 00:32:32.900
What can it tell you,
what can't it tell you?

00:32:32.900 --> 00:32:36.220
I think it's worth taking
a moment to understand

00:32:36.220 --> 00:32:39.510
what it's capable of and what
your need is for going deeper

00:32:39.510 --> 00:32:42.360
and having a different
solution than what you have.

00:32:42.360 --> 00:32:45.020
Explore scenarios in which fake content

00:32:45.020 --> 00:32:46.693
might damage your reputation.

00:32:47.556 --> 00:32:49.480
People that work in public affairs

00:32:49.480 --> 00:32:51.580
or issues management positions,

00:32:51.580 --> 00:32:53.000
I'm sure you have an understanding

00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:57.020
of places where Yale might be perceived

00:32:57.020 --> 00:32:58.970
as vulnerable or at risk.

00:32:58.970 --> 00:33:01.050
What do those scenarios look like

00:33:01.050 --> 00:33:03.030
and then how do you game plan those out?

00:33:03.030 --> 00:33:05.870
Can you conduct crisis training

00:33:05.870 --> 00:33:08.090
to make sure that your response frameworks

00:33:08.090 --> 00:33:11.030
are actually something that's workable

00:33:11.030 --> 00:33:12.700
and are not gonna fail you

00:33:12.700 --> 00:33:14.870
when the moment to deploy
them actually comes.

00:33:14.870 --> 00:33:16.910
And how do you create
muscle memory among staff

00:33:16.910 --> 00:33:20.240
who maybe are not familiar
with crisis management

00:33:20.240 --> 00:33:22.900
and you want them to know
how they're going to react

00:33:22.900 --> 00:33:25.250
in the moment when
something like this happens.

00:33:28.410 --> 00:33:30.560
I'm doing fantastic on
time, this is great.

00:33:31.800 --> 00:33:33.723
So, last one.

00:33:35.850 --> 00:33:38.320
This is a new idea so you
guys are my guinea pigs here.

00:33:38.320 --> 00:33:40.420
I have not presented this anywhere before.

00:33:41.520 --> 00:33:43.430
What I'm calling personal ecosystems

00:33:43.430 --> 00:33:45.510
as the new executive equity.

00:33:45.510 --> 00:33:47.640
And if executive equity is

00:33:47.640 --> 00:33:50.260
too agency jargony of a term for you,

00:33:50.260 --> 00:33:53.640
it basically just means how do we promote

00:33:53.640 --> 00:33:57.900
and raise the profile of
executives within an organization

00:33:57.900 --> 00:34:00.083
through PR, marketing, advertising.

00:34:02.560 --> 00:34:03.910
So, I've mentioned this before

00:34:03.910 --> 00:34:06.440
but there really is a strong trend

00:34:06.440 --> 00:34:09.260
towards consumers wanting CEOs

00:34:09.260 --> 00:34:11.350
to be engaged on social right now,

00:34:11.350 --> 00:34:14.800
and CEOs starting to hop on social.

00:34:14.800 --> 00:34:17.170
We're seeing that consumers

00:34:17.170 --> 00:34:18.590
are more likely to trust companies

00:34:18.590 --> 00:34:21.570
when they see that their
CEO is active on social

00:34:21.570 --> 00:34:24.020
and responding to issues about the company

00:34:24.020 --> 00:34:26.430
or things that are happening in the news.

00:34:26.430 --> 00:34:29.810
We see that employee engagement
and satisfaction is higher

00:34:29.810 --> 00:34:32.713
in companies where CEOs
are engaging in those ways.

00:34:34.870 --> 00:34:37.140
And again the consumers are more likely

00:34:37.140 --> 00:34:39.610
to want to associate and make purchases

00:34:39.610 --> 00:34:41.760
with companies that they
feel are taking a stand

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:44.060
and where they see that active engagement,

00:34:44.060 --> 00:34:45.840
particularly from the C-suite.

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:48.930
And all of this really
points to over the next year

00:34:48.930 --> 00:34:52.920
an influx of even more
CEOs and C-suite executives

00:34:52.920 --> 00:34:55.660
on social media that
are on there right now,

00:34:55.660 --> 00:34:57.860
particularly on the
platforms like Twitter,

00:34:57.860 --> 00:34:59.920
Facebook and LinkedIn.

00:34:59.920 --> 00:35:01.200
And everyone will tell you

00:35:01.200 --> 00:35:03.460
you need to have a strategy
for that, and you do.

00:35:03.460 --> 00:35:06.550
And my response is, yeah,

00:35:06.550 --> 00:35:09.983
but social is not really
the greatest place anymore.

00:35:11.170 --> 00:35:12.670
It's a bit of a dumpster fire.

00:35:14.120 --> 00:35:17.460
Is that where you want your CEO to engage?

00:35:17.460 --> 00:35:22.460
Do you want your CEO exposed
to all of the craziness

00:35:22.530 --> 00:35:24.120
and the people that are gonna
come out of the woodwork

00:35:24.120 --> 00:35:25.360
and attack?

00:35:25.360 --> 00:35:29.640
It's not really a conducive
forum to complex discussions,

00:35:29.640 --> 00:35:30.890
as we've found right now.

00:35:32.160 --> 00:35:34.260
And the other thing is I
think a lot of executives

00:35:34.260 --> 00:35:36.540
who will naturally take to the platform

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:38.070
are probably early adopters

00:35:38.070 --> 00:35:39.720
and they're probably already there,

00:35:39.720 --> 00:35:43.490
so I do have my own doubts
about whether or not

00:35:43.490 --> 00:35:45.810
executives that are
coming on late to the game

00:35:45.810 --> 00:35:47.910
are going to be comfortable in the medium

00:35:47.910 --> 00:35:49.280
and really put themselves out there

00:35:49.280 --> 00:35:51.230
and say things that are interesting enough

00:35:51.230 --> 00:35:55.150
to get the kind of traction
that they're hoping to get

00:35:55.150 --> 00:35:56.763
through a social program.

00:35:59.090 --> 00:36:01.823
Example number one of course is Elon Musk.

00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:06.810
A totally native user of social,

00:36:06.810 --> 00:36:11.040
has used it to essentially
be a one-man PR shop

00:36:11.040 --> 00:36:13.870
that has vastly increased the reputation

00:36:13.870 --> 00:36:15.220
of all of its companies.

00:36:15.220 --> 00:36:18.940
But of late we've literally seen him

00:36:18.940 --> 00:36:21.000
destroy shareholder value with a tweet,

00:36:21.000 --> 00:36:23.120
so a bit of a cautionary tale as well

00:36:23.120 --> 00:36:25.600
and again something that I think

00:36:25.600 --> 00:36:27.770
executives and anyone who's charged

00:36:27.770 --> 00:36:30.070
with putting together an
executive equity program

00:36:30.070 --> 00:36:31.970
really needs to think about carefully.

00:36:34.090 --> 00:36:37.240
At the same time, I am
completely fascinated

00:36:37.240 --> 00:36:40.550
by the emergence of slow media.

00:36:40.550 --> 00:36:43.330
I think there's a really
interesting creative tension

00:36:43.330 --> 00:36:45.660
happening right now in
the media landscape.

00:36:45.660 --> 00:36:50.040
So much is pushing towards faster,

00:36:50.040 --> 00:36:54.220
more snackable, more
visual types of content.

00:36:54.220 --> 00:36:58.360
All of the advertising
and monetary incentives

00:36:58.360 --> 00:37:00.380
of the media system are aligned

00:37:00.380 --> 00:37:03.830
with pushing for that
faster type of content.

00:37:03.830 --> 00:37:07.220
And I think because of all the challenges

00:37:07.220 --> 00:37:08.450
we're having social right now

00:37:08.450 --> 00:37:10.463
and relative lower trust in the media,

00:37:11.370 --> 00:37:13.490
people want to pull back from that.

00:37:13.490 --> 00:37:16.920
They don't necessarily want to
engage in all of those things

00:37:16.920 --> 00:37:19.040
as much as they did in the past.

00:37:19.040 --> 00:37:20.420
And I think we're starting to see

00:37:20.420 --> 00:37:22.520
the development of media systems

00:37:22.520 --> 00:37:27.520
that are tailored more
towards slower consumption,

00:37:27.690 --> 00:37:30.400
deeper engagement on individual topics

00:37:30.400 --> 00:37:32.820
and I think this is why
we're seeing newsletters

00:37:32.820 --> 00:37:36.010
have a resurgence in popularity
over the last few years.

00:37:36.010 --> 00:37:38.440
They're both able to
cut through the clutter

00:37:38.440 --> 00:37:39.730
of all of the different things

00:37:39.730 --> 00:37:41.840
that are coming in on your social feeds

00:37:41.840 --> 00:37:44.700
and give you a curated view
of what is most important.

00:37:44.700 --> 00:37:47.100
And a lot of the most
successful newsletters

00:37:47.100 --> 00:37:49.620
don't try and boil the
ocean and cover everything,

00:37:49.620 --> 00:37:51.800
they go really deep on one topic.

00:37:51.800 --> 00:37:54.100
Like Casey Newton's

00:37:56.130 --> 00:37:57.650
newsletter for The Verge

00:37:57.650 --> 00:37:59.770
that specifically covers Facebook

00:37:59.770 --> 00:38:01.393
and tech and privacy issues.

00:38:02.390 --> 00:38:05.040
There's an amazing blog slash newsletter

00:38:05.040 --> 00:38:07.990
that is about business and
tech called Stratechery.

00:38:07.990 --> 00:38:11.630
These are all made for
people who want to spend

00:38:11.630 --> 00:38:15.030
a lot more time and thought
on a particular topic.

00:38:15.030 --> 00:38:17.300
And I think nowhere
are we seeing this more

00:38:17.300 --> 00:38:21.230
than in the rise of
podcasting as a format.

00:38:21.230 --> 00:38:24.340
So, podcasting seems like
it's gonna keep growing

00:38:25.210 --> 00:38:26.700
really fast year over year.

00:38:26.700 --> 00:38:29.180
The ad dollars are starting to
come there as well right now

00:38:29.180 --> 00:38:31.460
which bodes well for
the health of podcasting

00:38:31.460 --> 00:38:33.260
as an overall media ecosystem.

00:38:33.260 --> 00:38:35.360
And as you can see from the chart here,

00:38:35.360 --> 00:38:37.580
the amount of time people
are spending with podcasts,

00:38:37.580 --> 00:38:39.900
which is a very intimate format

00:38:39.900 --> 00:38:41.870
that allows you to go really deep,

00:38:41.870 --> 00:38:45.340
really thoughtfully, slowly
on an individual subject,

00:38:45.340 --> 00:38:48.710
that's also climbing really fast.

00:38:48.710 --> 00:38:52.420
Two hours per day people are
spending listening to podcasts.

00:38:52.420 --> 00:38:54.780
We're starting to get into TV territory

00:38:54.780 --> 00:38:57.930
in terms of how much of
your time is taking up,

00:38:57.930 --> 00:38:59.870
and that's a really great place

00:38:59.870 --> 00:39:02.663
for people who want to
get messages out there.

00:39:04.310 --> 00:39:06.830
And the way that all
of this comes together

00:39:06.830 --> 00:39:11.830
is the personal media ecosystem
for thought leadership.

00:39:12.620 --> 00:39:14.990
So, what do I mean by this?

00:39:14.990 --> 00:39:19.150
I think the best example is
just journalists themselves.

00:39:19.150 --> 00:39:20.510
Journalists can no longer afford

00:39:20.510 --> 00:39:25.510
to be employed by one news outlet

00:39:25.950 --> 00:39:28.690
where they're writing one column
or just covering one beat,

00:39:28.690 --> 00:39:30.540
they need to get themselves out there

00:39:30.540 --> 00:39:33.370
in many different formats because A,

00:39:33.370 --> 00:39:35.490
it increases their value
to the news organizations

00:39:35.490 --> 00:39:36.950
and helps them pull in more money,

00:39:36.950 --> 00:39:41.200
but it's also job security
and helps them find new jobs

00:39:41.200 --> 00:39:45.830
if the news outlet that they're
at goes under or cuts staff.

00:39:47.010 --> 00:39:49.170
They are able to build up personal brands

00:39:49.170 --> 00:39:50.450
across multiple channels

00:39:50.450 --> 00:39:53.210
that they're able to port
with them wherever they go.

00:39:53.210 --> 00:39:56.340
And Bill Simmons is a
fantastic example of this.

00:39:56.340 --> 00:39:59.240
He's pretty much the master at it.

00:39:59.240 --> 00:40:01.057
So, Bill Simmons, for
those who don't know,

00:40:01.057 --> 00:40:02.753
has made his name in sports.

00:40:04.760 --> 00:40:09.300
He has founded a wide
variety of media platforms.

00:40:09.300 --> 00:40:13.270
He has his own online
magazine called The Ringer.

00:40:13.270 --> 00:40:15.740
Before that he had one called Grantland.

00:40:15.740 --> 00:40:17.300
He has his own podcast.

00:40:17.300 --> 00:40:21.270
He has livestream, or he did live stream

00:40:21.270 --> 00:40:25.750
his analysis of Game of
Thrones after every episode.

00:40:25.750 --> 00:40:27.260
He has a Facebook Live

00:40:27.260 --> 00:40:29.530
that he puts on during sporting events.

00:40:29.530 --> 00:40:31.110
Six million followers on Twitter,

00:40:31.110 --> 00:40:34.540
177,000 followers on Instagram.

00:40:34.540 --> 00:40:39.340
He's got columns on multiple publications.

00:40:39.340 --> 00:40:40.770
Esquire, Recode.

00:40:40.770 --> 00:40:42.820
He had an HBO TV show for a while.

00:40:42.820 --> 00:40:47.130
He has extended his reach
through multiple channels

00:40:47.130 --> 00:40:49.400
and really built up a personal brand

00:40:49.400 --> 00:40:52.010
and connection with a
wide variety of audiences

00:40:52.010 --> 00:40:54.700
that goes really deep on
each of those channels.

00:40:54.700 --> 00:40:59.030
And I think this is potentially
a really interesting model

00:40:59.030 --> 00:41:01.410
for thinking about executive equity

00:41:01.410 --> 00:41:03.443
and thought leadership overall.

00:41:06.230 --> 00:41:10.770
So, this is Carla B. PhD, I made her up.

00:41:10.770 --> 00:41:12.320
She's not real.

00:41:12.320 --> 00:41:14.670
I tried to get an AI-generated fake person

00:41:14.670 --> 00:41:16.630
but the aspect ratios didn't work out,

00:41:16.630 --> 00:41:18.003
so, she's a stock photo.

00:41:19.030 --> 00:41:21.840
But you can imagine
that she is an employee

00:41:21.840 --> 00:41:26.670
at a biotech startup in Boston.

00:41:26.670 --> 00:41:29.040
She knows a lot about biology.

00:41:29.040 --> 00:41:31.580
She knows about startups
'cause she's in a biotech.

00:41:31.580 --> 00:41:33.250
She also cares deeply about policy

00:41:33.250 --> 00:41:34.530
because how policy is implemented

00:41:34.530 --> 00:41:36.680
directly affects her
ability to do her job.

00:41:37.530 --> 00:41:39.980
If you are charged with communications

00:41:39.980 --> 00:41:42.600
at the company that Carla works at,

00:41:42.600 --> 00:41:45.170
you could think about
all of the different ways

00:41:45.170 --> 00:41:49.650
you could raise her profile
and get her out there.

00:41:49.650 --> 00:41:52.950
You could be getting her
on podcasts as an expert

00:41:52.950 --> 00:41:56.410
to be interviewed on science podcasts,

00:41:56.410 --> 00:41:58.570
women in tech podcasts.

00:41:58.570 --> 00:42:00.800
You could be cultivating
her as an expert source

00:42:00.800 --> 00:42:03.780
with The Wall Street Journal
on the business side.

00:42:03.780 --> 00:42:05.810
She could have her own Twitter following.

00:42:05.810 --> 00:42:09.130
She could be a contributor to
a publication on neuroscience

00:42:09.130 --> 00:42:11.830
that exists on Medium called The Spike.

00:42:11.830 --> 00:42:16.830
She could be writing a series
of articles on Boston biotech

00:42:17.690 --> 00:42:20.400
on LinkedIn and cultivating
an audience there.

00:42:20.400 --> 00:42:22.720
She could be someone who
appears on Facebook Live

00:42:22.720 --> 00:42:23.980
during company events.

00:42:23.980 --> 00:42:25.750
And from a paid perspective,

00:42:25.750 --> 00:42:27.780
as you're thinking about sponsored content

00:42:27.780 --> 00:42:29.950
on podcasts or on newsletters,

00:42:29.950 --> 00:42:33.733
she could be a voice in those channels.

00:42:35.020 --> 00:42:38.420
So, I think what's interesting here is

00:42:38.420 --> 00:42:40.080
this is something that can be applied

00:42:40.080 --> 00:42:43.810
not just to a CEO or
not just to the C-suite.

00:42:43.810 --> 00:42:47.230
Expertise in companies
is not just locked up

00:42:47.230 --> 00:42:48.880
in those few individuals,

00:42:48.880 --> 00:42:51.310
it exists throughout the organization.

00:42:51.310 --> 00:42:53.390
She doesn't have to be
the primary spokesperson

00:42:53.390 --> 00:42:56.440
that is there on every
channel all the time,

00:42:56.440 --> 00:42:59.182
and in fact it's better if she's not.

00:42:59.182 --> 00:43:03.200
We have one client that I
have worked on for a while now

00:43:03.200 --> 00:43:05.050
where one of the challenges they had

00:43:05.050 --> 00:43:07.680
is they have a bunch of
different departments

00:43:07.680 --> 00:43:10.560
all of who have experts, all
of who get pitched to media,

00:43:10.560 --> 00:43:13.810
and because they all want the same media

00:43:13.810 --> 00:43:16.180
they're constantly stepping
over each other's pitches

00:43:16.180 --> 00:43:18.430
and it's actually working across purposes

00:43:18.430 --> 00:43:19.950
with their communication strategy

00:43:19.950 --> 00:43:22.250
and they're essentially
competing with themselves

00:43:22.250 --> 00:43:25.290
for the same space in news articles.

00:43:25.290 --> 00:43:28.560
If all the experts had
their own tailored plan

00:43:28.560 --> 00:43:30.993
for where they were being pitched,

00:43:32.050 --> 00:43:34.380
how that was happening
from an owned standpoint,

00:43:34.380 --> 00:43:37.080
from an earned standpoint,
from a paid standpoint,

00:43:37.080 --> 00:43:40.090
I think the reach of any
one particular individual

00:43:40.090 --> 00:43:41.190
would be smaller

00:43:41.190 --> 00:43:43.760
but you'd be able to
build up personal brands

00:43:43.760 --> 00:43:46.320
for all of these people
that run a lot deeper

00:43:46.320 --> 00:43:48.040
and you'd be able to have
a much more effective

00:43:48.040 --> 00:43:49.723
media presence overall.

00:43:51.910 --> 00:43:54.450
So, what does it mean for Yale?

00:43:54.450 --> 00:43:57.060
I think first and foremost, the basics.

00:43:57.060 --> 00:43:58.780
Are school leaders and spokespeople

00:43:58.780 --> 00:44:01.160
adequately represented on social?

00:44:01.160 --> 00:44:02.350
I stand by what I said about social

00:44:02.350 --> 00:44:03.910
not necessarily always
being the best place

00:44:03.910 --> 00:44:07.490
but it is sort of a
baseline that people expect.

00:44:07.490 --> 00:44:11.540
So, then, are you taking
advantage of slow media formats?

00:44:11.540 --> 00:44:14.530
And not to convey
content that is snackable

00:44:14.530 --> 00:44:16.570
but thinking about the long form

00:44:16.570 --> 00:44:18.570
really complex things you have to convey

00:44:18.570 --> 00:44:20.500
and the way that those can be vehicles

00:44:20.500 --> 00:44:22.620
for those types of communications

00:44:22.620 --> 00:44:25.400
in a way that standard
social channels cannot.

00:44:25.400 --> 00:44:27.300
And then if you are responsible

00:44:27.300 --> 00:44:29.850
for promoting a wide number of people,

00:44:29.850 --> 00:44:33.250
maybe it's all of the different
faculty in a department

00:44:33.250 --> 00:44:34.393
and their scholarship,

00:44:35.330 --> 00:44:37.370
what is the ecosystem strategy

00:44:37.370 --> 00:44:39.700
that you could build around
each of those individuals

00:44:39.700 --> 00:44:42.550
that want to work with you from
a communications standpoint

00:44:42.550 --> 00:44:45.300
to ensure that they're
all getting exposure,

00:44:45.300 --> 00:44:47.680
they're all getting the
right level of effort

00:44:47.680 --> 00:44:49.360
applied to their communications,

00:44:49.360 --> 00:44:50.840
and they're not stepping on each other

00:44:50.840 --> 00:44:53.960
trying to secure the same spot

00:44:53.960 --> 00:44:55.793
or talk to the same audiences.

00:44:58.920 --> 00:44:59.830
And that's it.

00:44:59.830 --> 00:45:02.189
I think we've got some time for questions.

00:45:02.189 --> 00:45:03.022
Thanks.

00:45:03.022 --> 00:45:06.022
(audience applauds)

00:45:08.933 --> 00:45:11.516
(gentle music)

